{"name":"Sun’s Glory","hex":"#f6f2e5","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sun","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Sundanese."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The star that is closest to the Earth."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sun"},{"term":"glory","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Great beauty and splendor.","Honour, admiration, or distinction, accorded by common consent to a person or thing; high reputation; renown."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To exult with joy; to rejoice.","To boast; to be proud."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glory"}],"entries":[{"title":"Glory (1989 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Glory is a 1989 American epic historical war drama film directed by Edward Zwick about the 54th Massachusetts Infantry Regiment, one of the Union Army's earliest African American regiments in the American Civil War. It stars Matthew Broderick as Colonel Robert Gould Shaw, the regiment's commanding officer and Denzel Washington, Cary Elwes, and Morgan Freeman as fictional members of the 54th. The screenplay by Kevin Jarre was based on the books Lay This Laurel (1973) by Lincoln Kirstein and One Gallant Rush (1965) by Peter Burchard and the personal letters of Shaw. The film depicts the soldiers of the 54th from the formation of their regiment to their heroic actions at the Second Battle of Fort Wagner."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/0a/Glory_%281989_film%29_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glory_(1989_film)"},{"title":"Sun","blocks":[{"text":"The Sun is the star located at the centre of the Solar System. It is a massive sphere of hot plasma, heated to incandescence by nuclear fusion reactions in its core, radiating the energy from its surface mainly as visible light and infrared radiation, with 10% at ultraviolet energies. It is the main source of energy for life on Earth. The Sun has been an object of veneration in many cultures and a central subject of astronomical research since antiquity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg/330px-The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Robert_Gould_Shaw.jpg/500px-Robert_Gould_Shaw.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Robert_Gould_Shaw.jpg/500px-Robert_Gould_Shaw.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARobert_Gould_Shaw.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Matthew_Broderick_2012.jpg/500px-Matthew_Broderick_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Matthew_Broderick_2012.jpg/500px-Matthew_Broderick_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMatthew_Broderick_2012.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Robert_Gould_Shaw_Memorial_%2836053%29.jpg/500px-Robert_Gould_Shaw_Memorial_%2836053%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Robert_Gould_Shaw_Memorial_%2836053%29.jpg/1280px-Robert_Gould_Shaw_Memorial_%2836053%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Robert_Gould_Shaw_Memorial_%2836053%29.jpg/500px-Robert_Gould_Shaw_Memorial_%2836053%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedi